<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide build settings.

    Two platforms share this tree — the Windows SAPI engine and the Linux daemon
    (docs/LINUX-PORT-PLAN.md) — so anything that must NOT drift between them
    belongs here rather than in an individual csproj.

    Deliberately does NOT set BaseOutputPath / BaseIntermediateOutputPath.
    build/pack-zip.ps1 copies from literal
    bin\Release\net10.0-windows\win-x64\publish paths; relocating the output tree
    breaks the packer's copy step silently and the ZIP gets composed from
    whatever was left in the staging folder. Tidier bin\ layout is not worth that
    failure mode.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR THE SHIPPED VERSION.

      build/pack-zip.ps1 (Windows) and build/pack-tar.sh (Linux, Phase 7) both
      read this value, so one number produces both artifacts. A per-script
      default is exactly how the two platforms end up shipping "the same"
      release under two different numbers — pack-zip.ps1 carried a hardcoded
      0.2.0 default for three releases past 0.2.0.

      This is the version being BUILT, not the last one shipped, and that
      changed on 2026-08-27 when CI started packing: the pack job runs
      pack-tar.sh with no -v on every push, so a default naming the last release
      would have CI produce artifacts named after a release that already exists
      — and a local run with no -v would overwrite the genuine one in dist/.
      So it is bumped when a version's work BEGINS, and a release run still
      passes -Version explicitly. Per CLAUDE.md the bump is a decision the user
      makes, never something the build infers.

      0.2.12 shipped 2026-08-30 (tagged v0.2.12) and is installed on the user's
      machine. This is the number after it.
    -->
    <VstVersion>0.2.14</VstVersion>

    <!--
      And every assembly in the tree carries it. Set here rather than per-project
      as of 2026-08-19, which REVERSES the scoping decision the daemon's csproj
      used to state: the Linux binaries stamped VstVersion, the Windows ones did
      not, so 0.2.8 shipped with a Control Panel whose About tab read
      "Version: 1.0.0.0" — the .NET default, unchanged since the first commit and
      wrong for eight releases.

      The old reasoning was that changing assembly versions on the shipping
      platform is not a side effect a shared props file deserves to have. True as
      far as it goes, and the cost of honouring it was a version number nobody
      could read off the product they were holding. It is now deliberate rather
      than a side effect: nothing binds against these versions (SAPI registration
      is path-keyed, none of the assemblies are strong-named), and the About tab
      reads them back off the shipped binaries so a stale engine left behind by
      an extract-over-the-old-folder shows up as a mismatch instead of silence.

      Consequence worth knowing: bumping <VstVersion> now changes every
      assembly's version, so a release run rebuilds everything. That is the
      intended behaviour — one number, one product.
    -->
    <Version>$(VstVersion)</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Lets a non-Windows box restore and build net10.0-windows projects. The
      targeting packs download on any OS; only COM hosting and the actual run are
      Windows-only. Without this, `dotnet build` of the Engine on the Mint
      development box dies at restore with NETSDK1100 before producing any
      diagnostic worth reading.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Opt-in strictness for platform-neutral projects (<VstPortable>true</VstPortable>)
    lives in Directory.Build.targets, NOT here.

    This file is imported at the TOP of every csproj, before the project sets its
    own properties, so a condition on VstPortable evaluates against an empty
    value and does nothing — quietly, with the build still green and the rule
    apparently in place. Do not move it back.
  -->

</Project>
